# Intent Tree

One node = one chapter of meaning = one file. The tree is the only truth about what the
system means. Node bodies are written in **English**.

## Edges — three kinds, never mixed

| Field | Meaning | Cycles |
|-------|---------|--------|
| `parent` | refinement: the child is a more concrete development of the parent | forbidden |
| `uses` | semantic dependency: a concept defined elsewhere in the tree | forbidden |
| `talks_to` | operational dependency: running parts exchanging data | allowed |

`uses` enters a slice as a **definition** (without it the node cannot be understood);
`talks_to` enters as **context**. A cycle in data is normal; a cycle in definitions is
always a bug.

## Identity

- Short id `iNNNN` is issued by `doc/intent/_registry.yaml` and **never** changes or is
  reused. It is what ADRs, commits and chat cite.
- `slug` is a human mnemonic and may be renamed; the file is `{id}-{slug}.md`.
- Path and depth are **derived** and live in the generated `MAP.md` / `INDEX.json`.
  Never store them in the node file — inserting a level would rewrite the whole subtree.
- Allocate ids only via `intent new`, only on the integration branch. For parallel work,
  reserve a block of ids in advance.

## Node front matter

```yaml
---
id: i0042
parent: i0007                # null only for the root
slug: schema
title: Relational schema
status: current              # proposed | current | superseded | retired
uses: [i0019]
talks_to: [i0031]
superseded_by: null
contracts:
  - id: c1
    text: "user_id is unique across the users table"
    enforced_by: "tests/db/test_schema.py::test_user_id_unique"
code_paths: ["src/db/models/"]
test_paths: ["tests/db/"]
open_questions: []
---
```

The front matter uses a **restricted YAML subset**: mappings, sequences of scalars,
sequences of mappings, quoted or bare scalars, integers, booleans, null. No anchors,
tags or block scalars — the validator rejects them instead of guessing.

There is no `updated_at`: git holds timestamps.

There is also **no realization state** — whether the project fulfils this node, who
approved it, why a proof went stale. That lives in `doc/intent/_realization.yaml` beside
the tree (see `07-realization.mdc`). Keeping it out is what stops one edit high in the
tree from rewriting every node file below it.

## Contracts

A contract is a statement that must hold **plus** the thing that enforces it.

| `enforced_by` | Meaning |
|---------------|---------|
| `path/to/test.py::test_name` | a test; the validator checks the file and symbol exist |
| `cmd: <command>` | a machine check run by the Grader |
| `review` | exception: no machine enforcer. Requires `reason` **and** Human approval |

A contract without an enforcer is a wish. `intent coverage` counts the exceptions; that
number must not grow quietly.

Contracts are inherited: a child may **tighten** a parent's contract, never loosen it.
Loosening starts at the parent and is always a Human decision.

## Body

```markdown
# <title>

## Refines            <!-- omitted on the root -->
One sentence: which part of the parent this node develops further.

## Meaning
Two to eight sentences. What it is and what it is for, without repeating the parent.

## Contracts
Explanation, when one line of front matter is not enough.

## Non-goals
What explicitly does not belong here.

## Open questions
Empty, or a list. An open question blocks implementation of the affected part.
```

`## Refines` and `## Meaning` are required. Over 120 body lines is a warning, over 200 an
error — bounded node size is what keeps slices small.

## Semantic axioms (judged by the Critic, not by the validator)

- **A1 Refinement** — the child is strictly more concrete; deeper means less abstract.
- **A2 Preservation** — every statement of the parent stays true of the child. If the
  child would contradict the parent, the change starts at the parent.
- **A3 New information** — the child does not restate the parent's definitions.
- **A4 Contract strengthening** — a child narrows or adds, never weakens.
- **A5 Path sufficiency** — identity follows only from the chain of ancestors; a foreign
  concept is pulled in through `uses`, never through a sibling.
- **A6 Frugality** — a node exists only where it carries a contract or meaning the parent
  does not hold. Not every class deserves a node.

Typical findings: *inversion* (the child's claim holds outside the parent's subject),
*false nesting* (the child could be a sibling without losing meaning), *skipping* (a
concept is used that is neither on the path nor in `uses`).

## Machine rules (validator)

V1 identity and registry · V2 single root, parent exists and is current, no cycles ·
V3 `uses` acyclic, edge targets exist · V4 a node owning `code_paths` declares a contract ·
V5 every contract has a valid enforcer · V6 `code_paths` overlap only along the ancestor
chain, the deepest node wins · V7 `superseded` points at its replacement, retired nodes
live in `_retired/` · V8 body sections and size · V9 generated files are current ·
V10 no `proposed` node on the default branch.

## Lifecycle

```
proposed  →  current  →  superseded (points at the replacement)
                      →  retired    (moved to _retired/, id frozen)
```

`proposed` lives **only on a working branch**; review is a normal git diff. `superseded`
and `retired` are never read as valid intent.

## Changing the tree

1. Find the highest node whose contract or meaning the request violates.
2. Write the delta on a branch (`proposed` nodes + a `change.md` naming ids, contracts,
   affected edges and the tests that will enforce the new contracts).
3. `intent validate` must pass, then the Critic (A1–A6), then the Human gate.
4. Promote to `current`, mark replaced nodes `superseded`, run `intent map`.
5. Only now may the code change. Mixing old and new intent in one implementation is
   forbidden.

Never "fix the code so the tree does not have to change" when the meaning changed.
